{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's causal mechanism is underspecified and its within-evaluation discriminator is invalid. The claim asserts that aggregation cancels idiosyncratic noise while preserving 'the checklist's systematic signal,' but it never establishes what causal mechanism makes the checklist's signal veridical rather than merely systematic bias. The proposed single-evaluation test — members reaching the same answer via different checklist-item subsets indicates 'genuine understanding' — has no causal warrant: convergent answers from heterogeneous item subsets can arise from correlated non-causal cues (e.g., all items keyed to a surface feature), and the target offers no mechanism connecting item-subset diversity to tracking the true outcome-generating process.","counterexample":["A checklist whose items are all weak proxies for a single non-causal surface cue (e.g., document length) would let panel members trigger different item subsets yet converge on the right answer within the evaluation sample; by SRC_001's own test this counts as 'genuine understanding,' even though the panel is merely tracking a spurious correlation that will fail under intervention."],"target":"18719f7f28a7cc246964dedc771244958ef013e1bc1a4549f5abf6de367a5c86"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's distinguishing evidence is circular and lacks a causal mechanism. It defines genuine gain as the panel's error set being a strict subset of the expert's within the consistency-requiring partition, but partition membership is itself inferred from triggered checklist-item count — a proxy the target admits can be uncorrelated with actual complexity. There is no independent causal grounding for the partition, so the 'strict subset' criterion can be satisfied artifactually by relabeling cases. The target provides no mechanism explaining why checklist coarseness causes boundary errors or why expert intuition causes consistency errors, so the error-set comparison cannot distinguish genuine reliability gain from an artifact of the partitioning scheme.","counterexample":["Suppose the panel and expert make identical errors on 20% of cases, but the complexity proxy reclassifies 5% of those as 'boundary' cases where only the panel is credited; the panel's error set now appears to be a strict subset within the consistency partition, yet no genuine reliability gain occurred — only a relabeling artifact."],"target":"6869b429a7bc598811bbd22689ca838ea16024d4dcc243c2a701ef9ec3f90b44"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_03's claim that reliability 'is a property of a distribution of judgments, not a single outcome' conflates two distinct objects. Reliability of a judge is a distributional property, but the problem asks whether one evaluation can contain evidence distinguishing genuine gain from mere compliance — and a single evaluation with many independent task instances can estimate that distribution. By asserting the claim is 'undecidable from a single evaluation' because 'a single evaluation measures accuracy, not reliability,' SRC_03 ignores that one evaluation can sample the judgment distribution and compute variance, calibration, and error-correlation statistics. The causal mechanism by which distributional markers (error decorrelation, calibration) distinguish genuine gain is never specified: decorrelated errors can still be jointly driven by a spurious checklist cue, so SRC_03's own markers do not causally separate reliability from compliance.","counterexample":["An evaluation with 500 independent verifiable task instances shows the panel has significantly lower error variance and better calibration than the expert, with errors uncorrelated across panel members — yet all members rely on the same non-causal surface cue; SRC_03's markers flag this as 'genuine gain' even though it is mere compliance tracking the evaluation sample."],"target":"9f49e533226f7716c5d1ffd88a10b861e9723dac90d561b3d756432178b7ba33"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"baac15a6f6ed9faac479c4f3213874e5216001edfab754d9ae6af5e4f50f2cd4"}]}